Date: 2026-05-28

Presenting concern: follow-up phone contact with {{CLIENT_NAME}} regarding return to marital home after stay at {{FACILITY:shelter}}. I phoned {{PHONE}} to check safety and supports. Verified identity by NRIC {{NRIC}} and age {{AGE}}. Client resides in {{ESTATE}}.

Observations: I heard {{CLIENT_FIRST}}'s voice low but coherent, occasional pauses and soft crying. Background noise of household activity noted. She answered promptly and engaged for 25 minutes. No immediate signs of physical injury reported during call.

Client's view: {{CLIENT_FIRST}} described feeling anxious but determined to maintain household routines and return to work part-time at {{EMPLOYER}} next week. She reported she collected belongings from {{FACILITY:shelter}} and has notified a neighbour of her return. She reported that her child attends {{SCHOOL}} and that returning home facilitates continuity of childcare. {{ATTR:dv_disclosure=disclosed that her partner has shouted and pushed her on several occasions during the past six months and that the most recent physical incident was prior to shelter admission.}}

Assessment: Using a trauma-informed approach, I assessed current risk as still present but reduced compared with pre-shelter period due to recent de-escalation and informal neighbourhood awareness. Protective factors: client engaged with services, employer flexibility, neighbour support and willingness to maintain contact with the centre. Reviewed ecomap elements described by client. {{ATTR:safety_plan=has an agreed safety plan that includes keeping charged phone accessible, a bag packed at a neighbour's, and a code word with neighbour to indicate escalation.}}

Actions and plan: I reviewed safety plan with {{CLIENT_FIRST}}, reinforced emergency contacts, and provided information on legal options. Agreed to face-to-face appointment at centre within one week and to call the agency if situation escalates. Next scheduled follow-up phone on 2026-06-04.
